<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waldemar, Margrave of Brandenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1280–1319</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Waldemar the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Waldemar the Great was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> from 1308 to 1319, the last important ruler of the Ascanian line. He brought the whole Mark under his rule and defended it against a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, Mecklenburg and other northern neighbours. His early death, followed in 1320 by that of the young Henry II, ended Ascanian rule and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> leaderless.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/waldemar-margrave-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Waldemar, Margrave of Brandenburg" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Unger (sculptor) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waldemar_Markgraf_Unger.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Waldemar was a son of Margrave Conrad of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stendal">Stendal</a> and of Constance, eldest daughter of the Piast duke Przemysł I of Greater Poland. He belonged to the line of Ascanian margraves that began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> in 1157. He was co-regent from 1302 and succeeded his uncle Otto IV as margrave in 1308. In 1309 he married his cousin Agnes, daughter of Margrave Hermann of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzwedel">Salzwedel</a>; the marriage remained childless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1307 an agreement with the Swienca family ceded him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> troops occupied Świecie, Tczew and the strategic places up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. King Władysław I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, who took Danzig, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-soldin">Treaty of Soldin</a> of 1309 Waldemar gave up his claims east of the Łeba to the Teutonic Order for 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks. The same year Rostock shut its gates against him. In 1312 he captured Margrave Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, who had to cede the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, Torgau and Großenhain and pay a ransom of 32,000 silver marks. In the imperial election of 1314 Waldemar voted for Louis IV against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a>.</p><p>His support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> against Prince Henry II of Mecklenburg and his occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lordship-of-stargard">Lordship of Stargard</a> provoked a coalition of Mecklenburg, Werle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His troops were defeated near Gransee in August 1316, and by the Treaty of Templin of 1317 he gave up Stargard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg">Neubrandenburg</a>, while Schlawe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> passed to Duke Wartislaw IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. On the death of his cousin John V in 1317 he became sole ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and in 1319 he acquired Züllichau and Schwiebus in Silesia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Waldemar died on 14 August 1319. When his ward Henry II died in 1320, the margraviate fell as a vacant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> to King Louis IV, who in 1323 granted it to his eldest son Louis, ignoring the claims of the Ascanian princes of Anhalt. In 1348 an impostor in the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> claimed to be Waldemar returning from a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. Exploiting the rivalry of the Wittelsbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> dynasties, he was invested by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> for about two years before he was unmasked and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waldemar, Margrave of Brandenburg-Stendal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mieszkowice, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.79,14.49&amp;year=1305" aria-label="Mieszkowice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Waldemar, Margrave of Brandenburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5742 5534h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5742 5534h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.79,14.49&amp;year=1305">What was here in 1305? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
